import{_ as e,o as r,c as o,ag as s}from"./chunks/framework.2cFhd8Ux.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Rubrics","description":"","frontmatter":{"source":"assessment/rubrics.md"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"assessment/rubrics.md","filePath":"assessment/rubrics.md"}'),n={name:"assessment/rubrics.md"};function a(d,t,i,h,g,c){return r(),o("div",null,[...t[0]||(t[0]=[s('<h1 id="rubrics" tabindex="-1">Rubrics <a class="header-anchor" href="#rubrics" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Rubrics&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Scoring guides for every graded component except the oral exams (<a href="./oral-exams.html#scoring">Oral Exams: Scoring</a>), the quizzes (<a href="./quizzes.html#scoring">Due-Date Quizzes: Scoring</a>) and the graduate work (<a href="./../graduate/">graduate/</a>). Each row is something a grader can see in a repository, a CI run, a PR or your writing.</p></blockquote><p><strong>How to read these tables.</strong> Each rubric has criteria (rows) and four levels (columns). The number in bold is the points for that level.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Exemplary</strong></td><td>Everything asked for, done carefully, with evidence anyone can check. Beyond what most students do</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Proficient</strong></td><td>Everything asked for, done well. <strong>This is a good result</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Developing</strong></td><td>Started, but important parts are missing, unverified or unclear</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Missing</strong></td><td>Not there</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Graders pick the level that best fits each row. If a row falls between two levels, they use the lower one and say what would lift it. Weights and how points turn into your grade are in the <a href="./#how-grades-are-calculated">assessment README</a>.</p><p><strong>Three rules apply to every rubric.</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Evidence, not assertion.</strong> &quot;Tests pass&quot; earns credit when there&#39;s a CI run, a command and its output, or a commit a grader can check out. Claims about code, agents and tools are tagged <strong>observed</strong> or <strong>inferred</strong>; an inferred claim presented as fact is a Developing-level mistake.</li><li><strong>Honest limitations never cost points.</strong> A known issue you name is worth more than one a grader finds. A documented blocker (the exact error, what you tried, the fallback you switched to) earns partial credit where the rubric says so.</li><li><strong>Rubric levels describe the work, not misconduct.</strong> Work that seems to break the AI-use rules or seems fabricated is handled under <a href="./#academic-integrity">Academic integrity</a>, and no score changes until the University process is complete.</li></ol><hr><h2 id="labs-and-assignments" tabindex="-1">Labs and assignments <a class="header-anchor" href="#labs-and-assignments" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Labs and assignments&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>35% of the course for CS [4xxx], 25% for CS [5xxx].</strong> Weeks 1–13 are each scored out of <strong>20 points: the Lab checkoff (5) and the take-home assignment (15)</strong>. Your lowest week is dropped. <a href="#the-pr-round">The PR round</a> adds 20 points and is never dropped. Each week&#39;s <code>lab.md</code> ends with an assignment section whose <strong>rubric summary</strong> says what each row below means that week (for example, &quot;Verification&quot; is precision and recall against the defect key in week 9, and Cohen&#39;s κ in week 12).</p><h3 id="lab-checkoff-5-points" tabindex="-1">Lab checkoff (5 points) <a class="header-anchor" href="#lab-checkoff-5-points" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Lab checkoff (5 points)&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Staff check the week&#39;s in-lab deliverable during the Lab&#39;s checkoff block (or earlier, if you finish the core early) and record <strong>complete</strong>, <strong>partial</strong> or <strong>missing</strong>. The criteria are at the end of the lab&#39;s Core parts. Staff check the evidence (a passing test run, a trace line, a commit, a diff) and ask one question about it. <strong>Oral exam weeks:</strong> if your exam slot falls in your Lab, show the checkoff in office hours that week instead, for full credit; it isn&#39;t an absence (week 8; week 14&#39;s checkoff is feedback only).</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Complete</th><th>Partial</th><th>Missing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>5</strong> · Every checkoff criterion met, with the evidence on screen, and you can answer the question about it</td><td><strong>3</strong> · Most criteria met, or met but you can&#39;t yet answer the question; or blocked, with the exact error and what you tried</td><td><strong>0</strong> · Not shown, or absent without arrangement (<a href="./#absences-and-make-up-work">make-ups</a>)</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="assignment-15-points" tabindex="-1">Assignment (15 points) <a class="header-anchor" href="#assignment-15-points" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Assignment (15 points)&quot;">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Exemplary</th><th>Proficient</th><th>Developing</th><th>Missing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Deliverables work</strong></td><td><strong>5</strong> · Every deliverable in the assignment section works as specified, shown by tests, CI or a recorded run</td><td><strong>4</strong> · All present; one minor gap, stated in your write-up</td><td><strong>3</strong> · A major deliverable blocked, with the blocker documented; <strong>2</strong> · a major deliverable missing or broken and not mentioned</td><td><strong>0</strong> · Not submitted</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Verification</strong></td><td><strong>4</strong> · Checks decided before the code (the Verify line in <code>AI_LOG.md</code>); tests that fail without the change, shown once; adequacy measured where the week asks for it (properties, mutation score, precision and recall, κ); claims tagged observed or inferred</td><td><strong>3</strong> · Tests pass and cover the main behavior and at least one edge case; adequacy reported where asked</td><td><strong>1</strong> · Happy-path tests only, or adequacy not measured where asked, or claims untagged</td><td><strong>0</strong> · No tests or checks</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Process record</strong></td><td><strong>3</strong> · An <code>AI_LOG.md</code> entry with Specify · Delegate · Verify and what your checks caught; small commits with clear messages and <code>Assisted-by:</code> trailers on AI-assisted ones</td><td><strong>2</strong> · Log entry and trailers present, with minor gaps</td><td><strong>1</strong> · One large commit, or a log that lists tools but not what was verified</td><td><strong>0</strong> · No log and no meaningful history</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Write-up</strong></td><td><strong>3</strong> · The week&#39;s report or README section answers every question, each point located (file and line, commit, command and output); limitations stated</td><td><strong>2</strong> · Answers most questions; some points not located</td><td><strong>1</strong> · Vague or generic</td><td><strong>0</strong> · Missing</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Stretch work</strong> (the lab&#39;s &quot;If time&quot; parts and any extension) is optional and never lowers a score.</p><p><strong>Week 9&#39;s review freeze.</strong> Your five reviews and the AI reviewer&#39;s output are graded as they stood at your freeze (default 11:59 pm two days after the Lab; grace tokens don&#39;t extend it), and the defect key is released to you only after it (<a href="./../weeks/09-review-at-scale/lab.html#deliverables">week 9</a>).</p><hr><h2 id="the-pr-round" tabindex="-1">The PR round <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-pr-round" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The PR round&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>20 points, individual, counted in Labs and assignments and never dropped.</strong> For the rules, the ring and the dates, see <a href="./../project/pr-round.html">The PR Round</a>. Grace tokens don&#39;t apply: the round runs on a fixed clock.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Exemplary</th><th>Proficient</th><th>Developing</th><th>Missing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Author: the change</strong></td><td><strong>4</strong> · Fixes an <code>eswa-pr-round</code> issue in your assigned fork; small and focused; tests that fail without it; CI green on the PR; follows the receiving team&#39;s <code>AGENTS.md</code> and conventions</td><td><strong>3</strong> · Works and is tested; CI green; slightly broader than the issue</td><td><strong>1</strong> · Untested, CI red, or unrelated to the issue</td><td><strong>0</strong> · No PR opened in the round</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Author: description and disclosure</strong> (🔴 AI off)</td><td><strong>3</strong> · Written by you: what, why, how you verified it, risks; AI section complete; <code>Assisted-by:</code> trailers; issue linked</td><td><strong>2</strong> · All parts present; one thin</td><td><strong>1</strong> · Generic or missing the AI section</td><td><strong>0</strong> · No description</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Author: response to review</strong></td><td><strong>3</strong> · Every review comment answered within the round, with a change or a reasoned reply</td><td><strong>2</strong> · Most answered</td><td><strong>1</strong> · Acknowledged without acting</td><td><strong>0</strong> · No response</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reviewer: located and accurate</strong> (🔴 AI off)</td><td><strong>4</strong> · Every point names a file and line; claims checked by running the code or tests (observed); no invented problems</td><td><strong>3</strong> · Most points located; claims mostly checked</td><td><strong>1</strong> · General comments (&quot;LGTM&quot;, &quot;looks fine&quot;), or at least one problem that isn&#39;t there</td><td><strong>0</strong> · No review</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reviewer: useful</strong></td><td><strong>3</strong> · Most important issue first, with a suggested fix; blocking and non-blocking comments marked; one genuine strength</td><td><strong>2</strong> · A real issue and a suggestion</td><td><strong>1</strong> · Only praise, or only nitpicks</td><td><strong>0</strong> · Unusable or unkind</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reviewer: decision</strong></td><td><strong>3</strong> · Your team&#39;s decision (merge, request changes or decline) recorded on the PR with its reasons, within the round, politely</td><td><strong>2</strong> · Decision recorded; reasons brief</td><td><strong>1</strong> · Decision without reasons, or after the round closed</td><td><strong>0</strong> · No decision</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Invented problems cost points in review, as they do in professional review: an AI reviewer that flags things that aren&#39;t there is noise, and so is a person.</p><hr><h2 id="team-project-milestones" tabindex="-1">Team project milestones <a class="header-anchor" href="#team-project-milestones" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Team project milestones&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>35% of the course for CS [4xxx], 30% for CS [5xxx]; 130 points: M0 15, M1 30, M2 40, Final 45.</strong> Deliverables and dates are defined in <a href="./../project/milestones.html">Team Project Milestones</a>. Every milestone is graded from a snapshot of your team fork at the deadline (the commit of <code>main</code>, plus open branches and PRs). Rows marked <strong>Team</strong> are scored once for the team; rows marked <strong>Individual</strong> are scored for each member. Peer evaluation can adjust a member&#39;s share of the Team rows only (<a href="./#team-grades-and-peer-evaluation">how</a>).</p><p>Course documents in the team fork live in its <code>course/</code> folder (<a href="./../project/#where-things-live">why</a>).</p><h3 id="m0-proposal" tabindex="-1">M0: proposal <a class="header-anchor" href="#m0-proposal" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;M0: proposal&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>15 points, all Team.</strong> End of week 5.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Exemplary</th><th>Proficient</th><th>Developing</th><th>Missing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Scope and issues</strong></td><td><strong>4</strong> · A feature area in the class codebase and 3–6 issues (at least one per member), each with a reproduction or user story and a &quot;done when&quot;; why it fits in the term; an out-of-scope list</td><td><strong>3</strong> · Issues for every member with &quot;done when&quot;; feasibility argued briefly</td><td><strong>1</strong> · Vague area, or issues without &quot;done when&quot;</td><td><strong>0</strong> · Missing</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Spec</strong> (<code>course/SPEC.md</code>)</td><td><strong>4</strong> · Acceptance criteria in EARS form, every one checkable, including edge cases and constraints (compatibility, performance, no new dependencies); how each will be verified</td><td><strong>3</strong> · Checkable criteria for the main behavior and at least two edge cases</td><td><strong>1</strong> · Criteria that can&#39;t be checked (&quot;fast&quot;, &quot;robust&quot;)</td><td><strong>0</strong> · Missing</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Design and plan</strong> (<code>course/DESIGN.md</code>, <code>course/TASKS.md</code>)</td><td><strong>4</strong> · Affected modules named by file, observed from your onboarding; one alternative design considered; agent-sized tasks with owners and a Specify · Delegate · Verify line each; M1 and M2 targets; risks with a fallback</td><td><strong>3</strong> · Modules named; tasks with owners and verification; M1 target</td><td><strong>1</strong> · A task list without owners or verification</td><td><strong>0</strong> · Missing</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Team setup</strong></td><td><strong>3</strong> · <code>course/TEAM_CHARTER.md</code> current; <code>AGENTS.md</code> for the team fork; <code>course/THREAT_MODEL.md</code> for your agent setup; <code>main</code> protected (PRs with one approval); the README says how to build and test</td><td><strong>2</strong> · All present; one out of date</td><td><strong>1</strong> · Two or more missing</td><td><strong>0</strong> · None</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="m1-first-pr" tabindex="-1">M1: first PR <a class="header-anchor" href="#m1-first-pr" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;M1: first PR&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>30 points: Individual 18, Team 12.</strong> End of week 8. Each member&#39;s PR may be merged or open, as long as it has been reviewed.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Exemplary</th><th>Proficient</th><th>Developing</th><th>Missing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Individual: your PR&#39;s change and tests</strong></td><td><strong>8</strong> · Does what its issue says; tests that fail without it (link the failing run or commit); characterization tests where you touched untested code; CI green; a focused, reviewable diff</td><td><strong>6</strong> · Works; tested; CI green</td><td><strong>3</strong> · Partly works, thin tests, or CI red</td><td><strong>0</strong> · No PR</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Individual: description and disclosure</strong> (🔴 AI off)</td><td><strong>5</strong> · Written by you: what, why, how verified, risks; AI section complete; <code>Assisted-by:</code> trailers; issue linked</td><td><strong>4</strong> · All parts present; one thin</td><td><strong>2</strong> · Generic, or no AI section</td><td><strong>0</strong> · No description</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Individual: review loop</strong></td><td><strong>5</strong> · Reviewed by a teammate; every comment addressed with a change or a reasoned reply; you reviewed a teammate&#39;s PR with located comments (<a href="./../templates/REVIEW.html">REVIEW template</a>)</td><td><strong>4</strong> · Reviewed and addressed; your review given</td><td><strong>2</strong> · Review received or given, not both</td><td><strong>0</strong> · Neither</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Team: fork health</strong></td><td><strong>4</strong> · CI runs the test suite on every PR and is green on <code>main</code>; <code>main</code> protected; the fork builds in a fresh Codespace from the README</td><td><strong>3</strong> · CI on PRs; <code>main</code> green</td><td><strong>1</strong> · CI missing or red on <code>main</code></td><td><strong>0</strong> · The fork doesn&#39;t build</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Team: plan tracking</strong></td><td><strong>4</strong> · <code>course/TASKS.md</code> and issues current; the M2 target revised with evidence from M1 (what took longer, what the agent couldn&#39;t do)</td><td><strong>3</strong> · Tasks current; M2 target stated</td><td><strong>1</strong> · Plan not updated since M0</td><td><strong>0</strong> · No plan</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Team: AI log</strong></td><td><strong>4</strong> · <code>course/AI_LOG.md</code> has a Specify · Delegate · Verify entry for every M1 PR, with what verification caught and claims tagged observed or inferred</td><td><strong>3</strong> · Entries for most PRs</td><td><strong>1</strong> · Tool names only</td><td><strong>0</strong> · No log</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="m2-major-contribution" tabindex="-1">M2: major contribution <a class="header-anchor" href="#m2-major-contribution" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;M2: major contribution&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>40 points: Team 24, Individual 16.</strong> End of week 12.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Exemplary</th><th>Proficient</th><th>Developing</th><th>Missing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Team: the contribution</strong></td><td><strong>8</strong> · Implements the M0 spec as revised; a status table (met, partial, not met) that matches the code; works in the class codebase at the snapshot</td><td><strong>6</strong> · Main criteria met; status table honest</td><td><strong>3</strong> · Partial, and the status table overstates it</td><td><strong>0</strong> · No contribution</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Team: verification</strong></td><td><strong>8</strong> · Tests for every criterion, with property-based tests wherever the logic has invariants; mutation score on the changed modules (command, score, surviving mutants explained, equivalent ones identified); CI gates (tests, lint, CodeQL or Semgrep) green; no test skipped or deleted to get there</td><td><strong>6</strong> · Tests for the main criteria; mutation score reported; CI gates green</td><td><strong>3</strong> · Coverage only, or CI gates missing</td><td><strong>0</strong> · No tests</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Team: security review</strong></td><td><strong>4</strong> · The week 10 review applied to the contribution; findings fixed with regression tests or justified in writing; <code>course/THREAT_MODEL.md</code> updated; no secrets in the history</td><td><strong>3</strong> · Review done; findings handled</td><td><strong>1</strong> · Scans run but findings ignored</td><td><strong>0</strong> · No review</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Team: evals or design record</strong></td><td><strong>4</strong> · If the contribution uses an LLM: <code>course/EVAL_PLAN.md</code>, an eval set, a judge reported with percent agreement and κ, and a CI gate on recorded responses. If it doesn&#39;t: <code>course/DESIGN.md</code> updated with two decisions, the alternatives rejected and the evidence for each</td><td><strong>3</strong> · Evals or design record present; one part thin</td><td><strong>1</strong> · Mentioned but not done</td><td><strong>0</strong> · Missing</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Individual: your PRs</strong></td><td><strong>8</strong> · At least one substantive PR you authored that is part of the contribution, with tests, your own description and disclosure</td><td><strong>6</strong> · A substantive PR, tested and disclosed</td><td><strong>3</strong> · Only small or untested PRs</td><td><strong>0</strong> · No PR</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Individual: your reviews</strong> (🔴 AI off)</td><td><strong>5</strong> · At least two teammates&#39; PRs reviewed with located comments; at least one real defect or risk found, or a reasoned approval</td><td><strong>4</strong> · Two reviews, located</td><td><strong>2</strong> · One review, or general comments</td><td><strong>0</strong> · None</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Individual: your record</strong></td><td><strong>3</strong> · Your M2 sessions in <code>course/AI_LOG.md</code>, with what was delegated and what your checks caught</td><td><strong>2</strong> · Entries present; verification thin</td><td><strong>1</strong> · Tool names only</td><td><strong>0</strong> · None</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="final-report-and-repository" tabindex="-1">Final: report and repository <a class="header-anchor" href="#final-report-and-repository" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Final: report and repository&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>35 of the Final&#39;s 45 points, all Team.</strong> The repository snapshot is your fork&#39;s <code>main</code> at the start of the final exam period, which staff tag <code>final</code>; the report is due on Canvas at the same time. The other 10 points are the <a href="#final-presentation">final presentation</a>. This follows UT Austin&#39;s rule: &quot;demonstrated behavior, reproducible checks, controlled engineering decisions, or honest limitations&quot;, not feature count (<a href="https://www.cs.utexas.edu/~witchel/378AC/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">CS 378</a>).</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Exemplary</th><th>Proficient</th><th>Developing</th><th>Missing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Demonstrated behavior</strong></td><td><strong>8</strong> · A grader can build the tagged snapshot from the README in a fresh Codespace, and the contribution works as the report says</td><td><strong>6</strong> · Builds; works with one gap the report names</td><td><strong>3</strong> · Builds only with help, or works less than claimed</td><td><strong>0</strong> · Doesn&#39;t build</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reproducible checks</strong></td><td><strong>9</strong> · One documented command or CI run reproduces the tests, mutation scores, scans and evals; every number in the report matches what it produces</td><td><strong>7</strong> · Most numbers reproducible</td><td><strong>3</strong> · Numbers with no way to reproduce them</td><td><strong>0</strong> · No checks</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Engineering decisions</strong></td><td><strong>6</strong> · Two decisions, each with the alternatives, the evidence and what you would watch for</td><td><strong>5</strong> · Two decisions with reasons</td><td><strong>2</strong> · Decisions stated without reasons</td><td><strong>0</strong> · None</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Honest limitations</strong> (🔴 AI off)</td><td><strong>6</strong> · Known issues, threats to the validity of your own numbers, and what you would do next; nothing in the repository contradicts them</td><td><strong>5</strong> · Known issues and next steps</td><td><strong>2</strong> · &quot;No known issues&quot;, or generic</td><td><strong>0</strong> · Missing</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Process and AI use</strong></td><td><strong>6</strong> · A summary of <code>course/AI_LOG.md</code>: what was delegated, what verification caught (with examples), what the agents couldn&#39;t do; the week 13 retrospective data; a contribution table by GitHub username (🔴 AI off)</td><td><strong>5</strong> · Summary, data and table present</td><td><strong>2</strong> · Thin, or the table contradicts the history</td><td><strong>0</strong> · Missing</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A team that didn&#39;t submit the week 14 draft loses 3 of these 35 points.</p><hr><h2 id="final-presentation" tabindex="-1">Final presentation <a class="header-anchor" href="#final-presentation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Final presentation&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>10 of the Final&#39;s 45 points, Team.</strong> Ten minutes per team with a live demo, in the scheduled final exam period. The full rubric with level descriptors is in <a href="./../project/final-presentation.html#rubric">The Final Presentation: Rubric</a>; this is the summary.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Points</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Demonstrated behavior: the contribution working live on the <code>final</code> tag</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>Reproducible check: one check rerun live, and its result matching the report</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Honest limitations: at least one real limitation stated without being asked</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table><hr><h2 id="reading-responses-and-seminar-participation" tabindex="-1">Reading responses and Seminar participation <a class="header-anchor" href="#reading-responses-and-seminar-participation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Reading responses and Seminar participation&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>7.5% of the course for CS [4xxx], 5% for CS [5xxx]:</strong> half of the quizzes, reading responses and participation component. It combines reading responses (60%) and Seminar participation (40%): <code>weight × (0.6 × responses ÷ 60 + 0.4 × participation ÷ 24)</code>.</p><h3 id="reading-response-5-points" tabindex="-1">Reading response (5 points) <a class="header-anchor" href="#reading-response-5-points" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Reading response (5 points)&quot;">​</a></h3><p>150–250 words, 🔴 AI off, on Canvas, due before the Seminar (default 11:59 pm the night before; week 1: before the Lab). Weeks 1–15, lowest three dropped. Graded on substance, not polish or grammar. This short rubric has three levels.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Full</th><th>Partial</th><th>None</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>The claim and its evidence</strong></td><td><strong>2</strong> · The reading&#39;s main claim, with at least one number or concrete finding, stated accurately</td><td><strong>1</strong> · A summary that misses the main claim, or gets a number wrong</td><td><strong>0</strong> · Not submitted</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Strength of the evidence</strong></td><td><strong>2</strong> · One specific question about the design, the sample, what was measured or how far it generalizes (&quot;who was studied, and would it hold for a 20,000-line codebase?&quot;)</td><td><strong>1</strong> · A general doubt (&quot;small sample&quot;) without saying why it matters</td><td><strong>0</strong> · None</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Your practice and a question</strong></td><td><strong>1</strong> · What it changes in how you would work, and one question for the Seminar</td><td>—</td><td><strong>0</strong> · Either part missing</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="seminar-participation-2-points-per-seminar" tabindex="-1">Seminar participation (2 points per Seminar) <a class="header-anchor" href="#seminar-participation-2-points-per-seminar" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Seminar participation (2 points per Seminar)&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Weeks 1–15, lowest three dropped (24 points count).</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Points</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>You took part: a contribution to the discussion or debate, a question, a demo volunteer; or, in a week with a graduate paper presentation, your question posted by the deadline (<a href="./../graduate/paper-presentations.html#questions-from-the-class">how</a>); or a substantive comment on the Canvas discussion within 24 hours of the Seminar (this counts too, so speaking up in the room is never required)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>Present, without taking part</td></tr><tr><td><strong>0</strong></td><td>Absent without arrangement. Recognized absences are excused (<a href="./#absences-and-make-up-work">make-ups</a>)</td></tr></tbody></table><hr><h2 id="peer-evaluation" tabindex="-1">Peer evaluation <a class="header-anchor" href="#peer-evaluation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Peer evaluation&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Submitted on Canvas, 🔴 AI off, with M1, M2 and the Final.</strong> Not scored by itself: a missing peer evaluation costs you 2 points of that milestone. What the ratings can do to a team score, and the safeguards, are in <a href="./#team-grades-and-peer-evaluation">Team grades and peer evaluation</a>.</p><p>Rate each teammate and yourself from 1 to 5 on each criterion, with <strong>one sentence of evidence</strong> per rating (a PR, a review, a meeting, a message). Ratings without evidence carry little weight.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>5 means</th><th>3 means</th><th>1 means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Does their share</strong></td><td>Took on a fair share of tasks and finished them, or said early when they couldn&#39;t</td><td>Finished most of their tasks, some late or partly</td><td>Rarely finished assigned tasks</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Quality of their work</strong></td><td>Their PRs were tested, disclosed and easy to review</td><td>Their PRs usually needed a round of fixes</td><td>Their PRs regularly broke things or were untested</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reviews others&#39; work</strong></td><td>Reviewed teammates&#39; PRs promptly, with located, useful comments</td><td>Reviewed when asked</td><td>Rarely reviewed, or only rubber-stamped</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Communicates and shows up</strong></td><td>Kept the team informed; came to agreed meetings or said in advance</td><td>Usually reachable; missed some meetings without notice</td><td>Hard to reach; often absent</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Helps the team decide</strong></td><td>Helped the team weigh options and resolve disagreements constructively</td><td>Took part in decisions</td><td>Disengaged, or blocked decisions without offering alternatives</td></tr></tbody></table>',57)])])}const m=e(n,[["render",a]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
